Closure handlers follow the rules in [Argument parsing and return values]($link.page('reference/registration/arguments')).

# [Creating a closure from a Zig function]($heading.id('create-from-zig-function'))

```zig
fn sum(a: i64, b: i64) i64 {
    return a +| b;
}

pub fn makeSumClosure(ctx: phpz.Ctx) !void {
    try ctx.call.expectNoArgs();
    phpz.closure.createFromFn(sum, ctx.retval);
}
```

Declare `function makeSumClosure(): \Closure` in the stub. PHP can store the result and call it as `$sum(1, 2)`.

`createFromFn` accepts typed signatures with an optional leading `Ctx` or `GuardCtx`. Its PHP closure declares by-value variadic arguments and no explicit return type. The handler checks arguments at invocation. Reflection sees the generic PHP declaration. The destination zval must be `undef` or `null`.

A handler can return `*phpz.zend.Reference` and PHP receives the referenced value. The generic `createFromFn` arginfo declares by-value variadic arguments, so reflection reports no by-reference return. For a real by-reference function, declare `function &name()` in the stub and return `*zend.Reference`. See [PHP references]($link.page('reference/types/references')) for creation and ownership. PHP 8.6 converts generic arginfo to runtime format at module startup.

A trailing `phpz.Variadic` collects positional and extra named arguments. Use `check()` to restrict their source and `Entry.expect()` to validate elements. Generic closures skip the Debug declaration-count assertion but validate actual arguments. Closures from registered functions or methods retain their original arginfo and run the assertion.

# [Creating a closure from function metadata]($heading.id('use-function-metadata'))

`zend.Function.createClosure(destination, options)` creates a closure from a registered function or method, preserving its signature and static variables. For instance methods pass `.object`. Initialize the destination to `undef` or `null` and keep the function metadata valid for the closure's lifetime.

For stateful closures, store state in a Zig-backed PHP object implementing `__invoke` and bind that method as the closure. See the `makeCounter` example.

# [Lifecycle]($heading.id('lifecycle'))

`createFromFn` cannot capture Zig locals. If the state object holds PHP values, copy and release them and report them to the GC. The `makeReference` example shows how to retain and return a reference.

`createFromHandler` accepts a native handler that must outlive the closure. Closure creation can bail out. See [Bailouts and cleanup]($link.page('advanced/bailout')).

See [Lifetime boundaries]($link.page('reference/types/ownership').ref('lifetime-boundaries')) for cleanup responsibilities across calls, requests, objects and module globals.
